 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting Gaussians by loss takes $O(N \log N)$, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Selection is $O(N)$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the list and sort it.
-*   **Performance:** Reduces the number of Gaussians by approximately 90% when combined with Hard Pruning, increasing rendering and training speed [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Maintains visual fidelity even with high pruning ratios (80%) [S1]. Integrates seamlessly into the standard 3D-GS training pipeline [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires specific iteration checkpoints (6000, 9000, 12000) [S1]. Effectiveness depends on the L1 loss converging quickly [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting the Gaussians by loss takes $O(N \log N)$, where $N$ is the number of primitives. This is performed infrequently (e.g., 3 times in a typical training run) [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces memory footprint by removing primitives. A pruning ratio of 80% can reduce the primitive count significantly, leading to faster rendering and training speeds [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Maintains Hessian approximation quality by removing only low-contribution primitives before opacity resets. Allows for aggressive pruning (80%) without visual degradation [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires tracking accumulated L1 loss per Gaussian. Timing is critical; incorrect placement in the pipeline degrades performance [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-Soft Pruning was introduced in the "Speedy-Splat" paper [S1, S2]. It was developed to address the need for faster rendering and training speeds by reducing the number of primitives without significant quality loss. The method leverages the observation that L1 loss becomes small after iteration 6000, except after opacity resets [S1].
+Soft Pruning was introduced in the context of "Speedy-Splat," a method for fast 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2]. It addresses the issue of Hessian approximation degradation as training progresses and L1 loss becomes small [S1]. By pruning before opacity resets, it ensures that the optimization landscape remains well-conditioned for the subsequent densification steps [S1].
 
